module Prelude where

data Eq (A : Type) : A -> A -> Type where
  refl : forall (a : A) -> Eq A a a

-- **Every datatype here is opened right where it is declared** (MS10 phase
-- 248). A `data` declaration puts the type and its constructors inside a
-- namespace of its own, so `refl` is `Eq.refl`; the prelude's namespace is
-- every modelled language's, and these are the short names that namespace is
-- expected to supply.
open Eq

-- A dependent pair whose two levels are its own, `Sigma` with every `Type₀`
-- written `Type` — so inference generalises it the way it generalises `And`
-- (MS3 phase 33c), one level per component and the result at their join.
--
-- **It is what the generated no-confusion family conjoins with where a
-- constructor's argument type mentions an earlier argument.** The paper's
-- telescopic equality states the later equation after transporting along the
-- earlier one (Cockx, Devriese and Piessens, §3.3), so the second component's
-- type mentions the first component's proof and `And` cannot hold it.
--
-- **No projections, deliberately.** The prelude's namespace is every modelled
-- language's, and nothing needs them: the elimination tactic builds the two
-- projections inline from the constructor, as it does for `And`.
data DPair (A : Type) (B : A -> Type) : Type where
  dpair : forall (a : A) (b : B a) -> DPair A B

open DPair

data Unit : Type where
  unit : Unit

open Unit

data Empty : Type where

data And (A : Type) (B : Type) : Type where
  both : forall (a : A) (b : B) -> And A B

open And

-- What generated substitution works over (MS6 phase 105): a language's free
-- variables are a list of names, and a simultaneous substitution is a list of
-- name-and-term pairs, the pair being `And`.
--
-- **`nil` and `cons` are names no object language may now use** — his choice,
-- 2026-09-21, knowing that the prelude's namespace is every modelled
-- language's (`ms6/CLOSEOUT.md` 3).
data List (A : Type) : Type where
  nil : List A
  cons : forall (a : A) (rest : List A) -> List A

open List

-- What a primitive comparison answers with (MS6 phase 97b).
--
-- **Not @Bool@, and the reason is the one namespace.** An object language
-- modelled in Thena routinely has its own booleans — @examples/canonical.thena@
-- declares @true@ and @false@ as constructors of TAPL's @Term@ — and a prelude
-- that took those names would collide with every such language. The two
-- constructors say what they are about instead, which is also what the
-- comparison rule means by them: the first for literals that agree, the second
-- for literals that differ.
data Comparison : Type₀ where
  same : Comparison
  different : Comparison

open Comparison

-- The three primitive types compare, and this is where the system is told so
-- (MS6 phase 97b). @declare-primitive@ takes the name and the type; it accepts
-- only names it has a reduction rule for, and checks the type is the shape the
-- rule reads — two arguments of the primitive's own type, answering with a
-- datatype of two constructors that take nothing.
--
-- **Nothing in the rule names @Comparison@**: it reads the declared type and
-- answers with that datatype's first constructor for equal literals and its
-- second for unequal ones, so a user who declares their own two-constructor
-- answer gets the same rule.
--
-- **The name is written and not quoted** (MS10 phase 245). A string literal in
-- a name position is a name at the *root*, and these four live in their type's
-- namespace — so `"String.eq"` is refused and `String.eq` is the name itself.
do declare-primitive String.eq (resolve-core core`String -> String -> Comparison`)
   ; declare-primitive Char.eq (resolve-core core`Char -> Char -> Comparison`)
   ; declare-primitive Int.eq (resolve-core core`Int -> Int -> Comparison`)
   ; declare-primitive String.append (resolve-core core`String -> String -> String`)

-- A decision with its evidence (MS6 phase 109a): either a proof, or a
-- refutation. It has two level parameters, its own and `Empty`'s, because
-- nothing is defaulted.
data Dec (A : Type) : Type where
  yes : forall (p : A) -> Dec A
  no : forall (n : A -> Empty) -> Dec A

open Dec

-- **Comparison with evidence** (MS6 phase 109a, his choice on
-- `ms6/CLOSEOUT.md` 32; a definition rather than a primitive since MS8 phase
-- 152). On two literals `decString a b` is `yes (refl String a)` or
-- `no ‹a refutation›`, and on a name nobody knows it is stuck — so a proof
-- that eliminates it gets `Eq String y x` in one branch and
-- `Eq String y x -> Empty` in the other. `String.eq` says only which, and that
-- is why a proof about a name it does not know could not use it. Generated
-- substitution decides with `decString`, so a proof about it follows the same
-- decision.
--
-- **This used to be a reduction rule in Haskell, and now it is three
-- definitions in this file.** That is what `trusted` is for: the escape hatch
-- is one core term whose claim is visible, instead of a primitive whose
-- behaviour only the implementation knows. What each `decP` rests on is
-- exactly two claims about its comparison primitive, and `:trust` names them.
--
-- **These are ordinary surface definitions, and that is the point** (his
-- ruling, 2026-10-14). They were written in the development calculus until
-- then, because a surface `trusted T e` leaves the type its body really has to
-- a hole and that hole could not be solved under a λ — which is where every
-- claim here sits. The defect was in the unifier, not in the surface language,
-- and it is fixed, so the file a reader opens to see what the system trusts is
-- written in the language they write their own proofs in.
--
-- **Why each type is ascribed `: Type₀`.** A surface type cannot say which level
-- a datatype application sits at — `Eq {0} String a b` is refused, a brace there
-- meaning an implicit *value* argument — so the level is inferred, and whatever a
-- declaration leaves undecided becomes a level parameter of the name. Elsewhere
-- in this file `A : Type` decides it, by tying every `Eq A …` to `A`'s own level;
-- these laws have no such parameter, being about `String`, `Char` and `Int` at a
-- fixed `Type₀`, and cumulativity then asks only that the level be at least 0. So
-- each `Eq` here would get a parameter of its own, offering a caller a choice that
-- cannot matter. The ascription decides it, and the stored type is exactly the
-- `Eq {0}` the development calculus wrote. **A body needs no ascription**: a level
-- in a term is inferred at the use.

-- **Transport along an equality**: given `e : Eq A x y` and a family `P`,
-- carry a `P x` to a `P y`. The one thing the decisions below need from `Eq`,
-- and the reason they need no trust of their own beyond the two laws.
transport : ∀ (A : Type₀) (P : A -> Type₀) (x : A) (y : A) -> (Eq A x y : Type₀) -> P x -> P y
transport = λ (A : Type₀) (P : A -> Type₀) (x : A) (y : A) (e : Eq A x y) (px : P x) -> (elim Eq (A) (λ (u : A) (v : A) (r : Eq A u v) -> P u -> P v) ((λ (c : A) (z : P c) -> z)) (x y) e) px

-- **Does this comparison say `same`?** `Unit` when it does and `Empty` when it
-- does not, which is how the two constructors are told apart: `isSame` applied
-- to `different` *is* `Empty`, so a proof that `same` and `different` are equal
-- transports a `unit` into one.
isSame : Comparison -> Type₀
isSame = λ (c : Comparison) -> elim Comparison () (λ (z : Comparison) -> Type₀) ((Unit) (Empty)) () c

-- **The two laws of `String.eq`, and the whole of what `decString` trusts.** Neither
-- is derivable: nothing in the type theory ties a comparison on an unknown
-- name to the equality type, which is what made closed-term preservation the
-- only way through before this existed.
--
-- `eqStringEqual` says a `same` answer means the two really are equal. Its body
-- is `refl String a`, honestly of type `Eq String a a`, and the claim is that it has
-- type `Eq String a b` — so on two equal literals the two types are convertible,
-- the node contracts away, and the claim is **discharged**. On a name nobody
-- knows it is **pending**, and on two literals that differ it is
-- **undischargeable**, which is right: you would have had to forge the `same`.
eqStringEqual : ∀ (a : String) (b : String) -> (Eq Comparison (String.eq a b) same : Type₀) -> (Eq String a b : Type₀)
eqStringEqual = λ (a : String) (b : String) (p : Eq Comparison (String.eq a b) same) -> trusted (Eq String a b) (refl String a)

-- `eqStringRefl` says the comparison answers `same` of a literal and itself.
-- Discharged at every literal, pending on a name — and it is what lets the
-- refutation below be honest.
eqStringRefl : ∀ (a : String) -> (Eq Comparison (String.eq a a) same : Type₀)
eqStringRefl = λ (a : String) -> trusted (Eq Comparison (String.eq a a) same) (refl Comparison same)

-- **`decString` itself trusts nothing.** It eliminates the comparison with a
-- motive that keeps the answer — `Eq Comparison (String.eq a b) c` — so each branch
-- is handed the evidence of which answer it is. The `same` branch passes that
-- to `eqStringEqual`; the `different` branch carries `eqStringRefl a` along the
-- supposed equality to get `String.eq a b ≡ same`, rewrites the `different`
-- evidence by it to get `same ≡ different`, and transports `unit` along
-- *that* into `isSame different`, which **is** `Empty`.
decString : ∀ (a : String) (b : String) -> (Dec (Eq String a b) : Type₀)
decString = λ (a : String) (b : String) -> (elim Comparison () (λ (c : Comparison) -> Eq Comparison (String.eq a b) c -> Dec (Eq String a b)) ((λ (p : Eq Comparison (String.eq a b) same) -> yes (Eq String a b) (eqStringEqual a b p)) (λ (p : Eq Comparison (String.eq a b) different) -> no (Eq String a b) (λ (q : Eq String a b) -> transport Comparison isSame same different (transport Comparison (λ (z : Comparison) -> Eq Comparison z different) (String.eq a b) same (transport String (λ (z : String) -> Eq Comparison (String.eq a z) same) a b q (eqStringRefl a)) p) (unit)))) () (String.eq a b)) (refl Comparison (String.eq a b))

-- **The two laws of `Char.eq`, and the whole of what `decChar` trusts.** Neither
-- is derivable: nothing in the type theory ties a comparison on an unknown
-- name to the equality type, which is what made closed-term preservation the
-- only way through before this existed.
--
-- `eqCharEqual` says a `same` answer means the two really are equal. Its body
-- is `refl Char a`, honestly of type `Eq Char a a`, and the claim is that it has
-- type `Eq Char a b` — so on two equal literals the two types are convertible,
-- the node contracts away, and the claim is **discharged**. On a name nobody
-- knows it is **pending**, and on two literals that differ it is
-- **undischargeable**, which is right: you would have had to forge the `same`.
eqCharEqual : ∀ (a : Char) (b : Char) -> (Eq Comparison (Char.eq a b) same : Type₀) -> (Eq Char a b : Type₀)
eqCharEqual = λ (a : Char) (b : Char) (p : Eq Comparison (Char.eq a b) same) -> trusted (Eq Char a b) (refl Char a)

-- `eqCharRefl` says the comparison answers `same` of a literal and itself.
-- Discharged at every literal, pending on a name — and it is what lets the
-- refutation below be honest.
eqCharRefl : ∀ (a : Char) -> (Eq Comparison (Char.eq a a) same : Type₀)
eqCharRefl = λ (a : Char) -> trusted (Eq Comparison (Char.eq a a) same) (refl Comparison same)

-- **`decChar` itself trusts nothing.** It eliminates the comparison with a
-- motive that keeps the answer — `Eq Comparison (Char.eq a b) c` — so each branch
-- is handed the evidence of which answer it is. The `same` branch passes that
-- to `eqCharEqual`; the `different` branch carries `eqCharRefl a` along the
-- supposed equality to get `Char.eq a b ≡ same`, rewrites the `different`
-- evidence by it to get `same ≡ different`, and transports `unit` along
-- *that* into `isSame different`, which **is** `Empty`.
decChar : ∀ (a : Char) (b : Char) -> (Dec (Eq Char a b) : Type₀)
decChar = λ (a : Char) (b : Char) -> (elim Comparison () (λ (c : Comparison) -> Eq Comparison (Char.eq a b) c -> Dec (Eq Char a b)) ((λ (p : Eq Comparison (Char.eq a b) same) -> yes (Eq Char a b) (eqCharEqual a b p)) (λ (p : Eq Comparison (Char.eq a b) different) -> no (Eq Char a b) (λ (q : Eq Char a b) -> transport Comparison isSame same different (transport Comparison (λ (z : Comparison) -> Eq Comparison z different) (Char.eq a b) same (transport Char (λ (z : Char) -> Eq Comparison (Char.eq a z) same) a b q (eqCharRefl a)) p) (unit)))) () (Char.eq a b)) (refl Comparison (Char.eq a b))

-- **The two laws of `Int.eq`, and the whole of what `decInt` trusts.** Neither
-- is derivable: nothing in the type theory ties a comparison on an unknown
-- name to the equality type, which is what made closed-term preservation the
-- only way through before this existed.
--
-- `eqIntEqual` says a `same` answer means the two really are equal. Its body
-- is `refl Int a`, honestly of type `Eq Int a a`, and the claim is that it has
-- type `Eq Int a b` — so on two equal literals the two types are convertible,
-- the node contracts away, and the claim is **discharged**. On a name nobody
-- knows it is **pending**, and on two literals that differ it is
-- **undischargeable**, which is right: you would have had to forge the `same`.
eqIntEqual : ∀ (a : Int) (b : Int) -> (Eq Comparison (Int.eq a b) same : Type₀) -> (Eq Int a b : Type₀)
eqIntEqual = λ (a : Int) (b : Int) (p : Eq Comparison (Int.eq a b) same) -> trusted (Eq Int a b) (refl Int a)

-- `eqIntRefl` says the comparison answers `same` of a literal and itself.
-- Discharged at every literal, pending on a name — and it is what lets the
-- refutation below be honest.
eqIntRefl : ∀ (a : Int) -> (Eq Comparison (Int.eq a a) same : Type₀)
eqIntRefl = λ (a : Int) -> trusted (Eq Comparison (Int.eq a a) same) (refl Comparison same)

-- **`decInt` itself trusts nothing.** It eliminates the comparison with a
-- motive that keeps the answer — `Eq Comparison (Int.eq a b) c` — so each branch
-- is handed the evidence of which answer it is. The `same` branch passes that
-- to `eqIntEqual`; the `different` branch carries `eqIntRefl a` along the
-- supposed equality to get `Int.eq a b ≡ same`, rewrites the `different`
-- evidence by it to get `same ≡ different`, and transports `unit` along
-- *that* into `isSame different`, which **is** `Empty`.
decInt : ∀ (a : Int) (b : Int) -> (Dec (Eq Int a b) : Type₀)
decInt = λ (a : Int) (b : Int) -> (elim Comparison () (λ (c : Comparison) -> Eq Comparison (Int.eq a b) c -> Dec (Eq Int a b)) ((λ (p : Eq Comparison (Int.eq a b) same) -> yes (Eq Int a b) (eqIntEqual a b p)) (λ (p : Eq Comparison (Int.eq a b) different) -> no (Eq Int a b) (λ (q : Eq Int a b) -> transport Comparison isSame same different (transport Comparison (λ (z : Comparison) -> Eq Comparison z different) (Int.eq a b) same (transport Int (λ (z : Int) -> Eq Comparison (Int.eq a z) same) a b q (eqIntRefl a)) p) (unit)))) () (Int.eq a b)) (refl Comparison (Int.eq a b))

data Sigma (A : Type₀) (B : A -> Type₀) : Type₀ where
  pair : forall (a : A) (b : B a) -> Sigma A B

open Sigma

fst : forall (A : Type₀) (B : A -> Type₀) (s : Sigma A B) -> A
fst = \ A B s -> elim Sigma (A B) (\ z -> A) ((\ a b -> a)) () s

snd : forall (A : Type₀) (B : A -> Type₀) (s : Sigma A B) -> B (fst A B s)
snd = \ A B s -> elim Sigma (A B) (\ z -> B (fst A B z)) ((\ a b -> b)) () s

andLeft : forall (A : Type) (B : Type) (c : And A B) -> A
andLeft = \ A B c -> elim And (A B) (\ z -> A) ((\ a b -> a)) () c

andRight : forall (A : Type) (B : Type) (c : And A B) -> B
andRight = \ A B c -> elim And (A B) (\ z -> B) ((\ a b -> b)) () c
